# Source: Life.md

## Key Entities
- **Life** (mental model) — Central entity; a universal conceptual tool for navigating complexity through pattern recognition.
- **Ray Dalio** — Peripheral; source of Principles.
- **Stephen R. Covey** — Peripheral; source of The 8th Habit.
- **Timothy Ferriss** — Peripheral; source of The 4-Hour Workweek.
- **Atul Gawande** — Peripheral; source of Being Mortal.
- **Ryan Holiday** — Peripheral; source of The Daily Stoic and Stillness Is the Key.
- **Mark Manson** — Peripheral; sources include Everything Is F_cked, The Subtle Art of Not Giving a F_ck, Will.
- **Tiago Forte** — Peripheral; source of The PARA Method.
- **Shane Parrish** — Peripheral; source of Clear Thinking.
- **Greg McKeown** — Peripheral; source of Essentialism.
- **Simon Sinek** — Peripheral; source of Find Your Why.
- **Brian Christian / Tom Griffiths** — Peripheral; source of Algorithms to Live By.
- **Kevin Kelly** — Peripheral; source of Out of Control.
- **Scott Galloway** — Peripheral; source of The Algebra of Happiness.
- **Nassim Nicholas Taleb** — Peripheral; source of The Black Swan.
- **David Allen** — Peripheral; source of Making It All Work.

None of these sources are currently dedicated wiki pages (except fragments like [[principles---ray-dalio]], [[being-mortal]], [[the-algebra-of-happiness---scott-galloway]], [[the-black-swan---nassim-nicholas-taleb]]). Most are either absent or only partially represented.

## Key Concepts
- **Life as a mental model** — Defined as a conceptual tool providing mental shortcuts for understanding patterns and relationships in complex situations. This is a meta-level abstraction.
- **Cross-source integration** — The model aggregates insights from 18 diverse sources spanning productivity, philosophy, decision-making, and mortality awareness.
- **Pattern recognition and relationship navigation** — Core mechanism of the model.

The "Life" mental model as described is essentially a meta-framework that does not yet exist in the wiki index. Related existing concepts include [[mental-models]] (implicit), [[planning]], [[effort]], [[tendency]], and [[humanity]] — but none capture this exact aggregation.

## Main Arguments & Findings
- **Core claim**: "Life" is a universal mental model that serves as a conceptual shortcut for making sense of patterns and relationships.
- **Support**: References 18 sources across domains (productivity, philosophy, decision science, mortality, digital organization).
- **Evidence strength**: The model is defined at a very high level of abstraction with no specific mechanisms, heuristics, or distinguishing features. It functions more as a category label than as a substantive model. Confidence is rated 0.7, suggesting moderate uncertainty.

